    "q-91252": {
      "dom_id": "step-q91252",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -9 & 5\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the right of ${a}_{11}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*",
      "worked_solution": "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the right of ${a}_{11}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 6 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -9 & 5\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+(-2){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{4}:={C}_{4}+2{C}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -8 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $1$st row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -8 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 9 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -8 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 5 \\\\ -8 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ 9 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3(3-45) \\\\  & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
