    "q-91232": {
      "dom_id": "step-q91232",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Calculate the value of \n$\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|.$\n\n*Hint: Use elementary column operations to make the entries to the left of ${a}_{44}$ equal to zero. You should be able to reduce the given $4\\times 4$ determinant to a $3\\times 3$ determinant.*",
      "worked_solution": "Using elementary column operations, let's make entries to the left of ${a}_{44}=1$ equal to zero.\n\nSince adding a multiple of one row (or column) to another row (column) in a matrix does not change the value of its determinant, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| &  & \\,\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:={C}_{1}+2{C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{2}:={C}_{2}+{C}_{4} \\\\ {C}_{3}:={C}_{3}+(-3){C}_{4}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpanding the result along the $4$th row, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,|\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 & -5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-1)^{4+4}|\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 1 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -5 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(49-25) \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
